---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import EvidenceBadge from '../components/EvidenceBadge.astro';
import { withBase } from '../lib/paths';
---

<BaseLayout title="Research">
  <span class="eyebrow">Method and open questions</span>
  <h1>Research</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    This site is a memoir on top and an archive underneath. Larry's memories are primary-source
    evidence — recorded exactly as given, never rewritten. Military and historical records are
    gathered alongside them. When the two disagree, both are kept, side by side.
  </p>

  <h2>The evidence scale</h2>
  <p>Every important claim carries one of five classifications:</p>
  <dl class="scale">
    <div>
      <dt><EvidenceBadge level="confirmed" /></dt>
      <dd>Established by contemporary documents or unambiguous records.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt><EvidenceBadge level="strongly-supported" /></dt>
      <dd>Multiple independent lines of evidence point the same way.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt><EvidenceBadge level="plausible" /></dt>
      <dd>Consistent with known facts; a working hypothesis, not a finding.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt><EvidenceBadge level="unverified-recollection" /></dt>
      <dd>Larry's memory, preserved as given, not yet matched to records.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt><EvidenceBadge level="contradicted" /></dt>
      <dd>Research disagrees with the memory. The memory is still preserved.</dd>
    </div>
  </dl>

  <h2>Asking Larry</h2>
  <p>
    The fastest research tool this project has is a conversation over breakfast. The family
    keeps a prioritized interview guide, photo by photo; his answers land on these pages in
    his own words.
  </p>

  <h2>The open questions</h2>
  <p>
    These six would open the most doors. The working ledger tracks seventeen; the rest are
    narrower, or are waiting on records already requested. Last reviewed
    <time datetime="2026-08-23">23 August 2026</time>.
  </p>
  <ol class="questions">
    <li>
      <strong>The killed-in-action report.</strong> In the autumn of 1965, convalescing at the
      Army hospital at Camp Zama, Larry was told that <em>Stars and Stripes</em> had reported
      him killed &mdash; and went looking for the paper. Find the item. No Hunnewell died in the
      entire Vietnam War, so any casualty listing under that name is itself the error. The
      newspaper databases and much of the <em>Stars and Stripes</em> archive have come back
      empty; what remains is casualty lists that have to be read by eye, and the Inglewood
      hometown paper, which is not digitised anywhere.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The unit.</strong> Larry remembers the 118th AOD &mdash; Aviation Operations
      Detachment. &ldquo;AOD&rdquo; turns out to be genuine period vocabulary, so the
      designation may be right and simply undocumented at his level. He places his radio van on
      the property of an 18th Aviation organisation, before 1st Cavalry aviation arrived &mdash;
      which would explain the remembered Cavalry association as succession rather than
      assignment &mdash; and remembers a 125th air traffic control unit taking over as he left.
      Identify the outfit he served with. See <a href={withBase('/units/')}>Units</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>&ldquo;Ton Son Nuht.&rdquo;</strong> Larry spelled it that way himself. Identify
      the headquarters base he remembers; T&acirc;n S&#417;n Nh&#7913;t is the leading hypothesis
      and is not yet confirmed. His spelling is phonetic, as you would expect of a name he only
      ever heard. See <a href={withBase('/places/thom-son-nuht/')}>the place entry</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sgt. Lowry / Lowery.</strong> Identify the Special Forces sergeant Larry remembers
      on top of N&uacute;i B&agrave; &#272;en &mdash; the man he ran around the old French fort to
      warn, on the day of the &ldquo;450 civilians&rdquo; accusation. The camp&rsquo;s call signs
      were boxing terms, a local scheme that appears in no published source; finding any sibling
      term may locate the detachment. See <a href={withBase('/people/sgt-lowry/')}>his entry</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Captain &ldquo;Guegler.&rdquo;</strong> Larry has identified him in a photograph
      and fixed what he did: the officer he called to get Rosethorn 007, the helicopter that came
      for him off the mountain. The name is remembered by ear &mdash; Guegler, Gutler, Getler.
      Identify him. See <a href={withBase('/people/captain-guegler/')}>his entry</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Bombs in the ocean.</strong> Larry remembers aircraft jettisoning bombs at sea.
      Establish whether that was routine procedure for a bombed-up aircraft that could not land
      heavy, a rule for aborted missions, a consequence of the political restrictions on targets,
      or period rumour &mdash; naval air operations manuals and Seventh Fleet records would
      settle it. This is the one question on this list with no research behind it yet.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Source priorities</h2>
  <p>
    Primary and contemporary sources first: Army and National Archives records, morning reports,
    operational and after-action reports, personnel records, contemporary <em>Stars and
    Stripes</em> issues, period maps and photographs, official casualty databases, and oral
    histories. Secondary histories inform context but do not override stronger contemporary
    evidence. Citations accumulate in <a href={withBase('/sources/')}>Sources</a>, and working
    notes live in the repository's <span class="record">research/</span> directory. A companion
    page catalogs the <a href={withBase('/research/resources/')}>public online resources</a> —
    free and paid — that can move each open question forward.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
  }

  .scale {
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .scale > div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 15rem 1fr;
    gap: 1rem;
    align-items: start;
    padding: 0.6rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .scale dd {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .questions li {
    margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 40rem) {
    .scale > div {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 0.3rem;
    }
  }
</style>
